  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nShortwave ridging was impacting the Central and Southern Plains \nearly today under mostly cloudy conditions. This ridge axis will \ncontinue to translate eastward as we move through the day while a \nvigorous mid/upper trough was moving into the Central Great Basin \narea. A 100 kt jet evident on 12Z H25 objectively analyzed upper air \nmaps was situated on the southeast periphery of the mid/upper trough \nextending downstream into the Central Rockies. This upper jet is \nprogged to weaken as it translates eastward over the Central Rockies \ntonight while we see a considerable increase in the low-mid level \n(H85-H5) flow tonight and Monday as the mid/upper builds into the \nRockies. A strong LLJ will transport moisture northward tonight \nsupporting convection over the high plains as it propagates eastward \nduring the predawn hours. Much if not all of this activity is \nexpected to be elevated or rooted above the robust low level \nshear/buoyancy. As we move into the early morning hours (3am-10am),\nwe could see some pockets of convection but much if not all of \nit is expected to be elevated in nature which may limit the \nthreat for more substantial strong or severe storms. It will be \nhard to rule out surface based convection as we move into the \nearly afternoon hours given unseasonable moisture in the pbl with\ndewpoints in the mid 60s as mid-lvl lapse rates begin to \nsteepen with the approaching mid/upper trough and with the \narrival of more robust large-scale forcing for ascent as the pv \nanomaly rotates into SW Kansas. The most likely time for sfc- \nbased storms appears to arrive after 2-3 PM along an eastward \nadvancing dryline in the vicinity of US 281 or between Hwy 14 \nand US 281. We continue to see considerable shear weighted in \nthe lowest 2 KM with large streamwise vorticity in the \ncyclonically curved hodographs on point soundings in south\ncentral KS. If we can manage to see a few breaks in the \nelevated convection and introduce any sfc heating, there could \nbe a window where sfc-based storms capable of damaging winds \nand a few tornadoes may materialize in addition to large hail. \nThe dryline is progged to be more of north to south oriented \nwhile the deep layer shear vectors are oriented southwesterly, \nso we could have a window where some discrete supercells are \npossible during the afternoon and early evening hours assuming \nwe get enough heating for some increase in low-lvl buoyancy. \nOnce again, the key thing will be the timing and amount of \ninsolation which will determine how unstable the airmass \nbecomes as we move through the afternoon hours. We eventually \nexpect to see a line of storms driving eastward into eastern KS \nby early evening hours with damaging winds becoming the primary \nhazard before exiting by 10-11 PM or shortly thereafter.\n\nSome post-frontal shower activity may linger overnight into Tuesday \nwhile a more stable post-frontal regime builds southward across\nthe region into Wed. Seasonable temperatures are anticipated \nWed-Thu with highs mostly in the 60s. Another compact and progressive\nshortwave trough may arrive across the area late Wed night into\nThu bringing some increasing probabilities for showers.\n\nShortwave ridging will return to the area on Friday with seasonably \nmild temperatures and dry weather conditions anticipated. \n\nAs we move into the weekend, a clipper system is progged to dig \nacross the Northern Plains driving a cold front southward over the \narea on Sat with some shower activity possible. We continue to see \nsome differences in how this system will track leading to low \nconfidence in precipitation chances while seasonable temperatures \npersist across the region.",
